{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-71798" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following sets are open?\n\n- $(-\\infty ,-2)$\n- $(0,2]\\cup [7,9]$\n- $[-5,-1]\\cup (2,4)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041801/state.json" "file_sha256" "141bde0041c39d803449904834c60426f768ff35fb92fd0134dea40f4810e38e"} {"question" "q-71798" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nAmong the given sets, only $(-\\infty ,-2)$ is open. The boundary point of the set is $-2$ and it does not lie in the set.\n\nThe other sets are not open because:\n\n- $(0,2]\\cup [7,9]$ has four boundary points $0$, $2$, $7$, and $9$, and the points $2$, $7$, and $9$ lies in the set.\n\n- $[-5,-1]\\cup (2,4)$ has four boundary points $-5$, $-1$, $2$, and $4$, and the points $-5$ and $-1$ lie in the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041801/state.json" "file_sha256" "141bde0041c39d803449904834c60426f768ff35fb92fd0134dea40f4810e38e"} {"question" "q-71798" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041801/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "51b1934120ab1bd3ae3df4cea8b39ccb912c5b929b335373f21a8f6d5ebd3aab"} {"question" "q-71798" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio 